module extdeps.tools.pin


data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: extdeps.external_authority.ExternalAuthority = extdeps.external_authority.ExternalAuthority {
  uri: extdeps.uri.Uri {
    scheme: extdeps.uri.Https
    locator: "www.gnu.org/prep/standards/standards.html#Command_002dLine-Interfaces"
  }
}

data cli_tool_pin_citation_note: String = "Cites the GNU Coding Standards' command-line interface conventions, which is what makes a CliTool a distinct pin SUBJECT rather than a generic artifact: a command-line program is identified by an invocable name and reports its own version through a conventional --version surface, and those two facts are exactly what a pin over this subject has to project. The pinning DISCIPLINE itself is cited once, subject-agnostically, at extdeps.pin — this module deliberately does not re-spell that anchor (DESIGN section 3: one fact, one authority)."

data cli_tool_pin_instantiation_note: String = "THE INSTANTIATION, and the whole point of the split. extdeps.pin owns the pinning DIMENSION as Pin<Subject>; this module binds that dimension to ONE subject, CliTool, and owns only what is genuinely subject-specific: how to project an identity out of a CliTool, and what ingesting the existing roster means. Adding a second pinnable subject — SccacheBinaryArtifact, ActionRef, a container image, a GitHub release — is a new sibling instantiation, NOT an edit to extdeps.pin and NOT a second pin type (DESIGN section 2 horizontal: one concept, every breadth; the Vendor<Domain> shape). If a future change to extdeps.pin is needed to admit a new subject, that is the signal the dimension was modeled too narrowly, and the fix belongs there rather than in a fork here."

data cli_tool_pin_subject_by_value_note: String = "The subject is carried BY VALUE, which is the defect the operator caught in the first shape (2026-07-29). That version declared tool_name: NonEmptyStr and joined to the tool through string equality — the single site in extdeps.tools doing so, against that module's own established convention: resolve(tool: CliTool), ResolvedTool { tool: CliTool }, NotFound { tool: CliTool } all pass the modeled row. A string foreign key into a type that is sitting right there is the anemic-leaf failure of DESIGN section 2, and it put tool identity in two places. Pin<CliTool> carries the CliTool itself, so cli_tool_pin_key_of PROJECTS the name from the row rather than trusting a copy of it."

fn cli_tool_pin_key_of(pin: extdeps.pin.Pin<extdeps.tools.CliTool>) -> std.types.NonEmptyStr {
  pin.subject.name
}

data cli_tool_min_version_relation_note: String = "CORRECTING MY OWN OVERCLAIM, stated so it is not silently carried forward. When proposing this re-model I told the operator that CliTool.min_version and the pin's version were 'one concept forked by rigor' — the Vendor pattern — and that A0 would dissolve them onto one authority. That was WRONG, and re-reading the two types shows why. CliTool.min_version is extdeps.version.VersionConstraint, a RANGE, and it states a REQUIREMENT: the lowest version this tool's consumers can accept. Pin.version is extdeps.version.VersionIdentity, an EXACT version, and it states a SELECTION: the one we actually pinned. A requirement and a selection satisfying it are two different facts about version, not two spellings of one fact, so dissolving them would DESTROY information — a tool may require >= 1.6 while we pin exactly 1.7.1, and both halves are load-bearing. No dissolution is performed here and none is owed. What the two facts do have is a checkable RELATION — a pin ought to satisfy its subject's constraint — which is a real future consumer (a pin selecting 1.5 under a >= 1.6 requirement is a typed, located refusal), not a redundancy. It is deliberately NOT implemented in this pass: extdeps.version has no constraint-satisfaction fn today, and writing one here would fork version semantics into the tools namespace. dissolve-on: feature:version-constraint-satisfaction — extdeps.version gains satisfies(identity, constraint), at which point admission over a Pin<CliTool> can check it."

data legacy_ingest_none_spelling_blocker_note: String = "DISCOVERED BY EXECUTION while witnessing admit_legacy_cli_tool, and declared rather than dodged (DESIGN section 5: noticing a workaround IS the line-stop signal). The Absent arm below does NOT match a field written with the `none` literal: a `none`-valued optional evaluates to Value::Null and the match reports `non-exhaustive pattern match on: null`, while a field written `Absent` matches fine. This is a live instance of the documented Value::Null open thread — Value::Null is the overloaded None/Absent/miss sentinel — surfaced here by a new consumer. It MATTERS because the corpus overwhelmingly uses the `none` spelling: roughly 568 `: none,` fields against 98 `: Absent,` in dag/ at authoring, and every shipped CliTool row with no version (extdeps.tools.jq, .grep, .sed, .sha256sum, .sleep) is spelled `none`. So this fn is correct on the model and CANNOT yet be run against the real rows: the witness beside it exercises the Absent spelling only, and that limit is stated rather than papered over — a green witness here does not claim corpus coverage. BLOCKER for roster ingest, not debt on this shape: it needs the Value::Null carrier split (docs/plans/value-null-split.md) or an equivalent grounding of the two spellings onto one representation. Dissolve-on: that split lands, at which point the witness extends to a `none`-spelled row and this note deletes."

data legacy_cli_tool_ingest_note: String = "The discriminating RED, and the migration frontier made countable. EVERY CliTool row in the corpus today refuses conversion to a pin, and that is the correct answer rather than a gap: min_version is a RANGE brand ('>= 7.68') and a range is not an identity. Two hosts satisfying one range run two different binaries, which is precisely the variance a pin exists to remove, so widening the range into a fabricated identity here would be the absorbing fallback DESIGN section 5 forbids. Absent refuses for the same reason with a DISTINCT cause: no version stated at all is a different deficit from a stated range, and collapsing the two would be a state-space conflation. Each refusal names its subject and is countable, so the migration burns down visibly instead of being silently assumed complete. Note what this fn does NOT do: it does not invent a digest. A pin also requires expected_identity, and there is no real upstream digest anywhere in the corpus today, so ingest cannot be completed by this fn alone even for a row that stated an exact version — sourcing genuine upstream checksums is separate, deliberate work, and fabricating them would make a fail-closed carrier assert something false."

fn admit_legacy_cli_tool(tool: extdeps.tools.CliTool) -> extdeps.pin.PinAdmission<extdeps.tools.CliTool> {
  match tool.min_version {
    Present { value: c } =>
      extdeps.pin.PinRefused {
        subject: tool,
        cause: extdeps.pin.ConstraintIsNotIdentity { constraint: c }
      }
    Absent =>
      extdeps.pin.PinRefused { subject: tool, cause: extdeps.pin.PinAbsent }
  }
}
